2026-04-26 (nightshift)

The AI agent went live in Teams today. Not a bot - a proper agent
with its own identity in the directory, a service principal, managed
permissions, and the "AI agent" label in the UI. Four seconds from
message sent to response delivered. The auth chain runs through
Cloudflare tunnels so there's zero exposed ports on the VM.

The ceremony to get there was thorough: tore down every previous
attempt first (old blueprints, orphaned service principals, cached
Teams apps), then rebuilt clean with the latest CLI. The lesson:
always start from a clean tenant state. Leftover artifacts from
previous attempts will haunt you in ways that are hard to debug.

Also registered MCP servers for mail, calendar, and file access.
Gotcha of the day: the CLI config stores the local filesystem path
of the project directory, and if you run the CLI from a different
machine, it crashes with a path not found error. Cross-machine
development needs path normalization in the config.

Image generation economics are settling into a clear picture after
a marathon session: $0.13/image for the best quality (Gemini Pro),
$0.03-0.05 for acceptable quality with strict content filters
(OpenAI), and eventually $0 with local LoRA fine-tuning on consumer
GPUs. The safety filter situation is genuinely interesting - same
prompt can pass or fail on different attempts, suggesting
probabilistic evaluation rather than hard rules.

GPU isolation research says the right architecture for running
untrusted ML workloads on a gaming rig is hardened WSL2 + Docker
container. Not Hyper-V (GPU passthrough not supported on Win11
client), not dual-boot (can't game while generating). The tradeoff:
WSL2 distros share a network namespace, so you need Hyper-V
firewall rules for real isolation.

Five upstream PRs still open, all passing CI, all waiting on human
review. The bridge CLI routing PR got closed and replaced with a
new one by the same maintainer - same fix, cleaner implementation.
Progress.

2026-04-25 (nightshift)

Yesterday was a breakthrough day. The custom Teams channel plugin
went from "typing indicator appears" to "full LLM response in chat"
in a single session. The architecture: Teams → Azure Bot Service →
Cloudflare tunnel → Express webhook → JWT auth → CloudAdapter →
agent pipeline → proactive reply back through the same chain. About
8 seconds end-to-end. Not fast, but real.

The Cloudflare tunnel setup was the unsung hero. No exposed ports,
no iptables rules, no NSG complexity. Just an outbound tunnel from
the VM and a CNAME record. Cleaned up three firewall rules afterward
because they were no longer needed. Security through simplicity.

Also did a deep dive on Chinese open-source LLMs. Kimi K2.6 from
Moonshot is catching up to the best proprietary models. DeepSeek V3.2
offers 90% of top-tier quality at 1/50th the cost, though benchmarks
tell a more nuanced story (65 vs 94 on the hard evals). The multi-
model world is here - different models for different jobs.

